@nsobid-4B159F0F7A360669738390EE8657B4F0
// Astral Chain v1.0.1: pre-rendered movies (the opening cutscene: 1600x900, 30 FPS, 8726 frames) keep their picture
// with their sound.
//
// The movie player's frame clock (run time 0x70007103E0; its clock, 0x7000710FB0, is the system
// tick in ms) re-anchored itself when the picture fell behind it by more than twice [player+0x64C] (66 ms): the start
// time [+0x644] was cleared and taken again from the frame then due, so the sound, which plays on by itself, stayed
// ahead by the whole delay for the rest of the movie. One stall over 132 ms (a slow first frame, an area loading)
// left 0.65-1.4 s between the voices and the lips in the opening cutscene, different at each start (measured
// against two Switch recordings). Here a late picture keeps its clock while it is
// less than 2 s behind: it is "due" until it has caught up, so the player decodes the next frames back to back and the
// picture rejoins its sound. Further behind, the player starts its clock again as before: the title screen's
// background movie starts its clock while the game is still loading, and catching that up held the title's "ZL + ZR"
// prompt back (19 s after the title appeared against ~4 s on a Switch, 2026-11-02).
//
// 0x7004E0 (run time 0x70007104E0; this file's offsets + 0x7000010000 are run-time addresses, as for the svc #0xB1
// of cutscene-catchup-1.0.1.pchtxt at 0x7000A1EFD4): ldr w8, [x19, #0x64c] (the threshold, compared doubled with how
// late the frame is) -> mov w8, #1000: a frame up to 2 s late is due (b.le 0x700071040C), a later one clears the
// start time.

@flag offset_shift 0x100

@enabled
007004E0 087D8052 // mov w8, #1000 (was ldr w8, [x19, #0x64c]): catch up to 2 s, else start the clock again
@stop
